  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>How a Declaration of Trust works in West Virginia</h2>
      <p>A declaration of trust is the written instrument that creates a trust. The settlor states an intent to hold property for named beneficiaries and appoints a trustee to manage it. Under West Virginia Code Chapter 44D (Uniform Trust Code), the instrument must be created by a written instrument executed with the same formalities as a deed under West Virginia law. Deeds require acknowledgment before a notary public or other officer authorized to take acknowledgments. The trust becomes active when it is funded: real property moves by a new deed to the trustee, and bank and brokerage accounts are retitled in the name of the trust or trustee. A successor trustee named in the document takes over if the acting trustee steps down, becomes incapacitated, or dies. When proof of the trust is needed, W.Va. Code § 44D-10-1013 lists what a certification of trust may contain, and a third party may require the trustee to furnish excerpts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Does West Virginia require notarization for a declaration of trust?</h3>
      <p>West Virginia requires trusts to be created by a written instrument executed with the same formalities as a deed under West Virginia law. Deeds require acknowledgment before a notary public or other officer authorized to take acknowledgments. The certification of trust under § 44D-10-1013 may be signed or otherwise authenticated by any trustee; no notary or witness requirement is specified for the certification itself. For real property transactions, the trust instrument or certificate of trust must be recorded.</p>
      <h3>Can West Virginia protect assets from the settlor's own creditors?</h3>
      <p>West Virginia does not have a specific self-settled domestic asset protection trust (DAPT) statute. The state follows the common law Rule Against Perpetuities unless modified by the trust instrument. Spendthrift provisions are recognized and enforceable under the Uniform Trust Code. Trust planning in West Virginia focuses on the state's favorable tax treatment (no estate or inheritance tax) and modern trust modification tools rather than specific asset protection legislation.</p>
      <h3>What goes in a certification of trust under W.Va. Code § 44D-10-1013?</h3>
      <p>The statute lists the contents: that the trust exists and the date the trust instrument was executed; the identity of the grantor; the identity and address of the currently acting trustee; the powers of the trustee; the revocability or irrevocability of the trust and the identity of any person holding a power to revoke; the authority of cotrustees to sign or otherwise authenticate and whether all or less than all are required to exercise powers of the trustee; the trust's taxpayer identification number; and the manner of taking title to</p>
      <h3>How does West Virginia treat business trusts?</h3>
      <p>West Virginia recognizes statutory trusts and business trusts under the West Virginia Uniform Statutory Trust Act, codified at W.Va. Code § 47-24-1 et seq. A statutory trust is a separate legal entity that can conduct business, hold title to property, and sue and be sued. Beneficial owners have limited liability similar to shareholders of a corporation. Common law business trusts (Massachusetts trusts) are also recognized, but the statutory trust framework provides greater certainty.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <p>Common issues include assuming West Virginia law does not require any notarization for trust creation (deeds require acknowledgment before a notary public, and the trust instrument should be executed with the same formalities as a deed), and leaving the trust unfunded so that property never actually moves into it through a new deed or retitled accounts. Another frequent mix-up is assuming West Virginia has DAPT legislation: the state does not have a specific self-settled asset protection trust statute. People also assume West Virginia has abolished the rule against perpetuities; the state follows the common law Rule Against Perpetuities unless modified by the trust instrument. Documents that omit a successor trustee, or that do not match the certification of trust described in § 44D-10-1013, create avoidable friction during administration.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>West Virginia notes</h2>
      <p>West Virginia operates under W.Va. Code Chapter 44D (Uniform Trust Code), including § 44D-10-1013 (Certification of Trust), § 44D-1-101 et seq. (General Provisions), § 44D-8A-801 et seq. (Uniform Directed Trust Act), and § 44D-8B-2 et seq. (Uniform Trust Decanting Act). The state levies no state estate tax and no state inheritance tax, making it tax-friendly for estate planning. However, West Virginia does impose state income tax on trust income, filed on Form IT-141. The state adopted the Uniform Trust Code with full UTC modification tools including nonjudicial settlement agreements, court modification for changed circumstances, and modification by consent. West Virginia follows the common law Rule Against Perpetuities unless modified by the trust instrument. The state has enacted both the Uniform Directed Trust Act and the Uniform Trust Decanting Act, providing comprehensive modern trust law tools. The statute explicitly preserves the separate memoranda of trust provisions under § 36-4A-1, creating a dual-track system for trust certification and recording.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

